{
  "dom_id": "step-q275332",
  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables, and suppose $\\text{E}[X∣Y]=2Y+1.$ If $\\text{E}[Y]=3,$ what is $\\text{E}[X]?$",
  "worked_solution": "We are given an expression for $\\text{E}[X∣Y]$ in terms of $Y,$ and we are asked to compute $\\text{E}[X].$ This is a direct application of the tower property, which states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[\\text{E}[X∣Y]].\n$$\n\nSo, we substitute the given expression for the inner expectation:\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[X]=\\text{E}[2Y+1]\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the linearity of expectation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =\\text{E}[2Y+1] \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{E}[Y]+\\text{E}[1] \\\\  & =2\\cdot 3+1 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
